      ],
      "text": "Most commercially available Wi-Fi access points use fixed antenna orientations that cannot be adjusted after installation. Consequently, the antenna radiation pattern remains the same regardless of the deployment environment. However, the optimal antenna orientation depends on the physical layout of the building and  the client locations. For example, in a multi-story home with a basement, an AP installed on the second floor should direct more energy downward, whereas an AP installed in the basement should provide stronger upward radiation. Because the antenna orientation is fixed, the resulting Wi-Fi coverage is often not optimized for the installation environment, leading to reduced coverage and performance.",
